56e8c0277f748a174d9\\\":[1,0,2]},\n  \\\"conclusion\\\":\\\"holds\\\",\n  \\\"defect_claim\\\":\\\"asserted\\\",\n  \\\"rationale\\\": (\\\"Current account. With the harness cadence corrected (one node ticked, drained and delivered at a time, so heartbeat replies land in the round that asked for them), the suspicion \\\"\n   \\\"holds and the earlier apparent refutation is explained as an artifact of the old cadence. Execution 092538c6a04e456e8c0277f748a174d9 (experiment 9d31a7bf98034ecaa9c70e2cef669b8c-experiment, \\\"\n   \\\"exit 0, both selected tests passed) parsed three events. Event 1 \"'`lost_accepted_window`: steps 500, resend_periods 100, dropped Accepted:1, produced BLE:675,Prepare:1,Promise:1,AcceptSync:1,\"\n   \"Accepted:2,AcceptDecide:1, after_drop BLE:667, accept_decide_total 1, leader_decided_idx 0, follower_decided_idx 0, follower_accepted_idx 1, follower_holds_undecided_entry true, \"\n   \"leader_still_stable true - i.e. exactly one client proposal, no ballot change, no protocol message at all after the deliberate drop beyond heartbeats, and the entry still undecided after 100 \"\n   \"resend periods even though the follower holds it and the leader is stable. Event 0 `delivered_accepted_decides` is the control: the same setup with the receipt delivered decides in 1 step \"\n   \"(leader and follower both at 1), so the non-decision is attributable to the lost receipt, not to the window or to the configuration. Event 2 `later_proposal_repairs`: a second proposal decides \"\n   \"both entries within 1 step (leader_decided_idx 2), which names the only enabled repair in that interval; the follower'\"'s decided index reads 0 there because the loop stopped as soon as the \\\"\n   \\\"leader decided and the Decide had not been delivered yet (a probe-side reading, not a target behavior). Source support for the responsibility: the library exposes resend_message_tick_timeout, \\\"\n   \\\"documented as 'the number of calls to tick() before a message is considered dropped and thus resent', and this snapshot's own runtime layer deliberately discards outgoing messages when its \\\"\n   \\\"buffer exceeds the cap, documenting that as safe 'because OmniPaxos already tolerates lost messages via its own resend/retry mechanisms' (omnipaxos_runtime/src/handle.rs:52-58). In the accept \\\"\n   \\\"phase that premise does not hold: the follower->leader direction has no retry at all, so the leader's quorum count stays one short of quorum until unrelated traffic or a ballot change repairs \\\"\n   \\\"it. Scope asserted: in a two-node configuration where the single dropped Accepted is exactly what the write quorum still needs, the quorum-accepted entry stays undecided for at least 100 resend \\\"\n   \\\"periods with no repair enabled by the protocol itself, and progress requires a new proposal or a leadership change. Counter-limitations kept in force: (a) the core API documents no bounded \\\"\n   \\\"decision time and its resend knob is a recommendation, so the finding rests on the library's own loss-tolerance claim plus this snapshot's intentional message dropping; (b) a client retry (the \\\"\n   \\\"runtime's documented 'retry safe' Timeout) effectively repairs the earlier entry as a side effect, so the lost duty is bounded by client retry behaviour rather than by the protocol; (c) only a \\\"\n   \\\"2-node configuration is probed, where one drop is quorum-critical - in wider clusters the same drop is masked whenever another follower's receipt still completes the quorum, and dropping all \\\"\n   \\\"needed receipts is a stronger fault assumption; (d) the window is 500 logical steps with an error-free, in-order transport.\\\"),\n  \\\"limitations\\\":[\n   \\\"Only the 2-node configuration is probed: the gap bites when the dropped receipt(s) are exactly those the write quorum still needs.\\\",\n   \\\"The earlier run's apparent repair (churn plus accept-sync re-advertisement) is real but requires a new Prepare/Promise cycle; this probe does not measure how often a deployment would incur one while no client traffic flows.\\\",\n   \\\"Client-side retry can repair the earlier entry as a side effect, so the practical impact depends on the caller's retry policy; the probe deliberately makes no further proposal.\\\",\n   \\\"The receipt's event 2 follower reading (decided 0) is a probe-side in-flight artifact, not a target behaviour.\\\"]\n }]\n}\njson.dump(d,open('update.json','w'),indent=2)\nprint('written')\nPY\ncd .. && /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -I -B -c \\\"import sys; sys.path.insert(0, '/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src'); from consensus_assurance.cli import main; raise SystemExit(main())\\\" validate --run . --submission update.json 2>&1 | tail -3\"","aggregated_output":"written\n{\"valid\": true, \"run_id\": \"463219b877cc44e3a3007f9a6a6cdb9e\", \"elapsed_seconds\": 2883.066309319991, \"diagnostics\": [], \"meaning\": \"Read-only validation of these bytes and saved state; no acceptance, execution or budget reservation\", \"no_change\": false, \"execution_requested\": true, \"effects\": [{\"object\": \"knowledge\", \"action\": \"create\", \"target\": \"knowledge[0]\"}, {\"object\": \"interpretations\", \"action\": \"append\", \"target\": \"interpretations[0]\", \"investigation_id\": \"1a5428f0aa284eb1a2a3f1a083fcb052-question-0\", \"changed_fields\": [\"experiment_id\", \"source_ids\", \"executions\", \"conclusion\", \"defect_claim\", \"rationale\", \"limitations\"]}, {\"object\": \"experiments\", \"action\": \"create\", \"target\": \"experiment\"}]}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"The cadence-corrected core probe settles the accept-phase question: execution 092538c6a04e456e8c0277f748a174d9 (exit 0, both tests pass) shows `lost_accepted_window` with only BLE traffic after the single deliberate Accepted drop (after_drop BLE:667), exactly one AcceptDecide in 500 steps (100 resend periods), leader_decided_idx 0, the follower holding the entry as Undecided with accepted_idx 1, and the leader still stable — while the delivered-receipt control decides in 1 step and a later proposal decides both in 1 step. So the earlier apparent refutation is explained as an artifact of the old tick-both-then-deliver cadence, which had provoked churn whose accept-sync path re-advertised the acceptance; the question now closes as conclusion holds with defect_claim asserted, scoped to the 2-node case where the dropped receipt is exactly quorum-critical, with the responsibility resting on the library's own loss-tolerance claim (resend knob; this snapshot's runtime dropping outgoing messages as 'safe because OmniPaxos already tolerates lost messages') and the limits kept (wider clusters mask a single drop; client retry effectively repairs; no bounded decision time is documented). A Behavior was promoted with that receipt. The update also extends the runtime probe with the cluster-level consequence that was previously only a limitation: a third case where only node 1 consumes events, nodes 2 and 3 park at event_capacity 8, and the healthy node 1 must be unable to complete a write (documented Timeout, decided index unchanged); all three cases pass locally.\"}"}}
